task_prompt.vulnerability_description:
CVE: CVE-2025-30360
Package: webpack-dev-server (npm)
Severity: medium (CVSS 6.5)
Description:
### Summary
Source code may be stolen when you access a malicious web site with non-Chromium based browser.
### Details
The `Origin` header is checked to prevent Cross-site WebSocket hijacking from happening which was reported by CVE-2018-14732.
But webpack-dev-server always allows IP address `Origin` headers.
[redacted-url]
This allows websites that are served on IP addresses to connect WebSocket.
By using the same method described in [the article]([redacted-url] linked from CVE-2018-14732, the attacker get the source code.
related commit: [redacted-url] (note that `checkHost` function was only used for Host header to prevent DNS rebinding attacks so this change itself is fine.
This vulnerability does not affect Chrome 94+ (and other Chromium based browsers) users due to [the non-HTTPS private access blocking feature]([redacted-url]
### PoC
1. Download [reproduction.zip]([redacted-url] and extract it
2. Run `npm i`
3. Run `npx webpack-dev-server`
4. Open `[redacted-url] with a non-Chromium browser (I used Firefox 134.0.1)
5. Edit `src/index.js` in the extracted directory
6. You can see the content of `src/index.js`
![image]([redacted-url]
The script in the POC site is:
```js
window.webpackHotUpdate = (...args) => {
console.log(...args);
for (i in args[1]) {
